# 8. PHASE FOUR — PREMIUM BIOLOGICAL PBR MATERIALS AND RENDERING

The Natural Anatomy appearance must become a functioning per-tissue rendering system.

Current tissue colours are a starting point. Complete the full material pipeline.

## 8.1 Per-tissue material architecture

Implement per-structure or per-tissue material properties, as appropriate:

- Base colour/albedo.
- Roughness.
- Metalness.
- Normal map.
- Optional bump/detail map.
- Appropriate opacity and transmission.
- Double-sided treatment only where anatomically justified.
- Surface-specific lighting response.
- Correct colour-space handling.
- Texture resolution and memory requirements.

For biological materials, metalness should normally remain zero or very close to zero unless a special non-biological annotation or visualization is being shown.

Use physically based materials rather than arbitrary highlights. Use environment lighting and a controlled studio-light setup. Preserve the educational clarity of the model.

Reference the official Three.js material documentation:

- https://threejs.org/docs/pages/MeshStandardMaterial.html
- https://threejs.org/docs/

## 8.2 Add authentic and appropriate surface detail

Where real assets provide textures, integrate them properly.

Where authentic textures are unavailable, use restrained procedural micro-detail only when it is scientifically and visually defensible.

Examples:

- Subtle cortical bone microstructure.
- Tissue-specific roughness.
- Muscle surface fibre direction only where its orientation is known.
- Subtle fibrous tendon and ligament appearance.
- Appropriate cartilage and adipose surface response.
- Organ-specific material differences.

Procedural visual detail must not be mistaken for microscopic anatomy, real histology, or actual surface morphology.

Do not apply the same generic noisy normal map to every organ.

Do not create fake pores, scars, vessels, muscle bundles, lesions or pathological changes to create a photorealistic appearance.

Where an asset lacks the correct UV mapping or geometry, fix the asset pipeline rather than forcing a texture onto it.

## 8.3 Make every quality preset functional

Fully wire the existing quality presets to actual renderer behavior.

The presets must control the appropriate combination of:

- Pixel ratio.
- Antialiasing strategy.
- Shadow quality.
- Environment and lighting quality.
- Texture resolution.
- Anisotropy where useful.
- LOD preference.
- Geometry memory.
- GPU texture memory.
- Post-processing cost.
- Rendering workload.
- Mobile constraints.
- Software-rendering fallback.

A preset must not simply change one UI label or pixel ratio while pretending to control everything else.

Provide an adaptive option that reacts to actual device capability and sustained performance. Ensure it does not oscillate rapidly between quality levels.

## 8.4 Rendering performance and lifecycle

Improve:

- Asset loading and unloading.
- GPU memory management.
- Texture and geometry disposal.
- Chunk caching.
- Context-loss handling.
- Resize behavior.
- Tab visibility behavior.
- Animation scheduling.
- Picking efficiency.
- Material reuse.
- Shader compilation.
- Mobile GPU fallback.
- Low-memory behavior.

Use on-demand rendering when appropriate and avoid continuous idle rendering where unnecessary.

Every viewer mount and unmount must dispose of its resources correctly. Avoid accumulating WebGL contexts, materials, geometries, event listeners, textures or animation loops.

## 8.5 Three.js version modernization

The current package previously used Three.js `^0.159.0` and matching older type definitions.

At the time of this directive, npm lists Three.js `0.187.0` as the latest stable release.

Assess the current version and migrate toward the latest stable compatible release using the official migration guide:

- https://www.npmjs.com/package/three
- https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/wiki/Migration-Guide

Migrate incrementally, respecting breaking changes. Keep Three.js and its type definitions compatible. Rework shader patches and renderer integrations wherever required.

Do not blindly replace working renderer code with a new API. Verify custom shaders, clipping, textures, picking, camera controls, quality presets and GPU resource handling after each migration step.

Do not adopt a WebGPU rewrite just for novelty. A stable, high-quality WebGL implementation with an optional future-compatible renderer architecture is preferable to a broken migration.

